    pr_ref:  # 40a3
    description: "FOUND WHILE WIRING WI-tohuk, 2026-08-03. Measured, and it inverts the\npriority that item implied.\n\nTHE NUMBERS, from load_builtin_taint_catalog() itself:\n    javascript   50 sources,  83 SINKS\n    typescript    6 sources,   0 sinks\n\nSo JavaScript is the largest unwired taint surface in the tree -- more sinks\nthan java (69) or rust (30) -- while TypeScript, which now has full DDG\nwiring, cannot move a single taint verdict because it has no sinks to reach.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING. All three layers are keyed to the string typescript:\n  * no cfg_nodes/javascript.yaml. typescript.yaml states in its own header\n    that it \"also covers JavaScript (same node types in\n    tree-sitter-javascript)\" -- but load_cfg_mapping is keyed by language, so\n    a .js file never reaches it. A comment that explains a boundary is a\n    decision input and decays like a filing (L50); this one reads as though\n    JavaScript were covered.\n  * ts_def_use registers via @register_def_use_extractor(\"typescript\") only.\n  * the LanguageDdgSpec globs *.ts.\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE: express (all .js) yields ZERO DDG edges, against\napollo-server (.ts) at 1176 edges / 91 symbols.\n\nWHY IT IS CHEAP. The grammars share node types -- that is what\ntypescript.yaml already asserts -- and ts_def_use._HANDLERS keys on\nlexical_declaration / variable_declaration / assignment_expression /\naugmented_assignment_expression / expression_statement, all present in\ntree-sitter-javascript. The work is registering a javascript key for the\nmapping, the extractor and the spec, NOT writing an extractor.\n\nDO NOT fold into another PR. It changes which flows exist for a language with\n83 sinks, so it needs its own 9-repo A/B (express and apollo-server are\nalready in the cohort) with the delta read per-flow against source rather\nthan tallied (L55).\n\nNOT COVERED BY THE NEW GATE, by construction: test_ddg_language_wiring\nquantifies over languages that HAVE a registered extractor, and javascript\nhas none, so nothing currently fails. That scope is declared in the gate\ndocstring rather than implied."  # 40a3
    fields: {}  # 40a3
- op: discuss  # c78b
  at: '2026-08-06T01:57:43Z'  # c78b
  by: agent  # c78b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c78b
  clock: 2  # c78b
  nonce: c78b  # c78b
  message: "DONE — PR #223, dev f93d16b42a. 21973 passed, 100% coverage, mypy ratchet clean at 672.\n\nWHAT LANDED. Three keys, no new logic: a second @register_def_use_extractor(\"javascript\") on the\nexisting TypeScript extractor class, a second LanguageDdgSpec(language=\"javascript\",\nfile_glob=\"*.js\"), and one entry in a new cfg._CFG_MAPPING_ALIASES pointing javascript at\ntypescript.yaml. The design fork was decided as the plan specified — alias, not a duplicated\njavascript.yaml — because typescript.yaml's own header already asserts the two share tree-sitter\nnode types, and copying the file duplicates the consequences of that assertion into a second\nartifact free to drift.\n\nMEASURED EFFECT, and its exact scope. On the recall-corpus fixture the\nhttp.createServer -> fs.writeFileSync flow moves from approximate/structural to precise/ddg: the\nwalk now runs on JavaScript and confirms the data dependence. THIS IS A FIXTURE, NOT A COHORT.\nTagged awaits_bakeoff_validation; the express / apollo-server A/B is still owed and must be read\nper-flow against source, not tallied.\n\nTHE PLAN'S \"REDDENS\" LIST WAS INCOMPLETE. It named test_dataflow_scope.py and\ntest_ddg_language_wiring.py. It did NOT name test_taint_recall_corpus.py, which contributed three\nfailures — and those were the interesting ones, because they used JavaScript as their exemplar of\na data-flow-INCAPABLE language. Wiring it vacated that role.\n\n  - Flipping those assertions to True would have left tests that distinguish nothing. Java takes\n    the role instead: it has a cfg mapping but no atomic_statement and no extractor, so blockers\n    is a strict subset and the PARTIAL case is exercised for the first time. Verified rather than\n    assumed that its fixture contributes ZERO evidence rows, so it cannot perturb a verdict.\n  - One test could not be fully repaired and says so in place.\n    test_adjudication_label_does_not_depend_on_another_language drew its power from the flow's own\n    language being incapable. JavaScript was the ONLY language that both produced a fixture flow\n    and lacked an extractor — java, c, php and ruby were each tried and every one yields zero\n    evidence rows, so there is no drop-in substitute. The invariant remains pinned at unit level\n    by test_taint.py::test_function_the_ddg_never_saw_is_structural_not_mixed, which builds the\n    shape synthetically (ddg_symbols={\"other_fn\"}) and cannot be invalidated by wiring a language\n    — a better home for the property than a fixture whose validity depended on a language staying\n    unwired. What is GONE is the end-to-end confirmation through a real incapable language.\n\nTWO SIDE FIXES, both justified on their own terms.\n  - register_def_use_extractor now stamps instance.language with the registration key. Stacking\n    two decorators constructs two instances, and without the stamp the second carried the first's\n    name — a Protocol attribute true only by class-author convention. No-op for every existing\n    single registration.\n  - test_cfg_mapping_declares_atomic_statement re-derived _CFG_NODES_DIR / f\"{language}.yaml\"\n    itself instead of asking production's loader — a second implementation of \"where does a\n    language's mapping live\", which broke the moment a mapping legitimately lived under another\n    key. It now calls load_cfg_mapping and reads the parsed field the CFG builder consumes.\n\nTWO FILING CLAIMS CORRECTED RATHER THAN PROPAGATED.\n  - \"JavaScript is the largest unwired taint surface in the tree\" is FALSE. By sink count it is\n    fifth: elixir 301, erlang 157, haskell 131, kotlin 93, javascript 83. The real argument is a\n    COST one — it was the only unwired language whose mapping and extractor already existed under\n    another key.\n  - The in-file apollo-server figure (588 DDG edges / 93 symbols) was measured on an older tree.\n    Marked for re-measurement in the comment rather than copied forward.\n\nSCOPE DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED: the glob is \"*.js\" only. .mjs / .cjs / .jsx are each a separate\nquestion about what discovery classifies as javascript, and widening a glob on an assumption is\nhow a spec starts lying about its own coverage.\n\nDocs updated with the code: CHANGELOG [Unreleased], docs/hypergumbo-spec.md (two places that\nenumerated the four extractors), ADR-0017 §3e phase-2 state row."  # c78b
